WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:09.290
Hello and welcome back to another
video with ItsDrDan, and today I'm

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:13.510
going to be explaining to you what
is chirality, especially looking

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.729
at enantiomers and diastereomers.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.810
And we're going to look at a bunch
of different examples as well to

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:21.950
try to explain what this concept is.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.570
Now what this all has to do with
is different carbons, just like

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:29.970
the one here, that have four
completely unique groups around them.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:34.269
So whenever there are four different
groups, what can happen is that when

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:37.870
it looks at its reflection, they
are completely different, right?

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:40.499
You are not able to superimpose them.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.290
Now what does that mean if
I can't superimpose them?

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:47.890
Well, let's say if I have my right
hand and I want to wave it at you.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.610
Now you might see like, Oh, wait a minute.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:51.990
That's not your right hand.

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.060
That's your left hand.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.050
Well, it's all about reflections, right?

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.570
Well, let's try to look at this in terms
of a camera to try to understand that.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:01.459
Let's use two of me to help explain this.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.380
So if I wave, let's say if I want to
wave my right hand, just like so, and

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:11.060
you could see, Hey, wait a minute,
they're both waving opposite hands.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.340
Well, this is the whole entire
idea as waving at your reflection.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.550
So if I use my right hand and I wave it
at my other reflection, notice how it's

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:23.580
waving its left hand side, and they're
both waving completely different hands.

00:01:24.059 --> 00:01:28.350
Well, if I take my hands as well,
and I try to look at them, and I

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.309
try to look at both your hands,
well, you can't overlap them.

00:01:32.745 --> 00:01:37.285
Meaning that if I put them on top
of each other, notice how my thumbs

00:01:37.605 --> 00:01:39.815
are in opposite positions, right?

00:01:39.905 --> 00:01:41.345
So you cannot overlap them.

00:01:41.955 --> 00:01:45.395
So they might be able to be
reflections of one another, right?

00:01:45.395 --> 00:01:50.385
So if I kind of show that my thumbs
are both reflecting, I can try to

00:01:50.385 --> 00:01:52.109
show how they're reflections this way.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.130
But I can't overlap them.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.420
It's the same idea as looking
at my reflection, right?

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.880
Both sides, we could be
waving at one another.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.030
We're both waving opposite hands.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.630
It's the same idea.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:06.440
Look at yourself in the mirror,
try to wave to yourself and you'll

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:08.379
notice it's the opposite side.

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:10.539
So what does this have
to do with chemistry?

00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:15.550
Well, if you have, let's say an
enantiomer or mirror images of each

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:19.739
other, they're each going to have
their own completely unique properties

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:21.480
behind each of these molecules.

00:02:21.935 --> 00:02:26.575
Especially if you look at things like in
biochemistry, where if you have proteins

00:02:26.575 --> 00:02:30.915
and enzymes and they have to bond to each
other in specific ways to allow a reaction

00:02:30.915 --> 00:02:34.964
to happen, well if it's not in the right
orientation, it's not going to bond.

00:02:35.294 --> 00:02:39.314
In fact, in some pharmaceutical
chemicals, right, one might be a drug

00:02:39.315 --> 00:02:43.125
that might cure something in your
body, the other one might be a harmful

00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:46.834
toxin, which is something that's
commonly found in the world around us.

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:50.970
So let's try to take a look at this
concept, we're going to be moving through

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.230
this and trying to understand this, so
let's look at some different examples for

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.670
me to try to explain chirality to you.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:02:58.590
Let's take a look.

00:02:58.869 --> 00:02:59.349
In

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:07.980
order for something to be chiral,
we have to first try to understand

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:13.190
not only just reflections but this
concept of four unique groups.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:17.290
So what I have here is I have a circle
drawn, and I have four different

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:21.350
colors, and in the middle of these
colors, we have an intersection.

00:03:22.019 --> 00:03:25.489
That intersection is your carbon, right?

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.839
So the carbon meets every
single different group.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:34.089
So if I have, let's say my mirror,
which I have drawn in blue, what

00:03:34.099 --> 00:03:37.040
exactly would that reflection be, right?

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.620
We have completely different mirrors.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.850
It's the same thing as looking
at the reflection of myself

00:03:42.929 --> 00:03:44.339
from earlier in this video.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:50.809
Now whenever we have two chiral carbons,
or sorry, two different chiral molecules.

00:03:51.415 --> 00:03:56.424
And we have their mirror images,
these are referred to as enantiomers,

00:03:56.774 --> 00:03:58.595
meaning that they are reflection.

00:03:58.595 --> 00:04:02.304
Notice how the light, the teals line
up, the blues line up, yellows line

00:04:02.304 --> 00:04:04.755
up, and reds go inside and outside.

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:08.225
The other thing that's pretty cool
about it is that, okay, well, if

00:04:08.235 --> 00:04:12.945
I take this thing, I can try to
overlap it as well, and I can see,

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:14.895
do those different colors line up?

00:04:15.394 --> 00:04:18.195
Now, one thing that we can
always try to do is, well, can

00:04:18.195 --> 00:04:19.754
I make it where they line up?

00:04:20.134 --> 00:04:24.645
And we can attempt to rotate this thing,
right, just like so, and I can try

00:04:24.645 --> 00:04:26.784
to see, like, well, does it line up?

00:04:26.815 --> 00:04:28.135
And it doesn't.

00:04:28.135 --> 00:04:28.735
There's no way.

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:30.345
We just start making different colors.

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:35.150
We can't make it line up, which
is something that's really, really

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:38.510
difficult when we're looking
at these types of molecules.

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.890
Whenever they are enantiomers, that means
that they are reflections of one another,

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:51.820
however, they are not superimposable,
meaning that you can not overlap them.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:56.859
So, not superimposable,
meaning no overlap.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.830
Now, when depending on the
molecules you have drawn, you

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.820
could have chiral molecules that
are identical to one another.

00:05:05.159 --> 00:05:08.239
that maybe are, let's say,
in a different arrangement.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:13.610
So let's say if I cut out this one on the
right and I re copy and paste the one on

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:18.110
the left, Well, if I have this structure,
sometimes the molecule just might be

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:20.030
in a slightly different orientation.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.380
So if you try overlapping it,
it might look like they are two

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:25.239
completely different things.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.720
You might say they're enantiomers.

00:05:27.269 --> 00:05:30.870
However, you need to make sure
you are rotating and looking at

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:35.520
molecules because sometimes They
can just be identical molecules.

00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:39.330
Notice how they're both chiral, but
in this case they are both the same.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.930
So we're going to look at
examples of that in a little bit.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.260
Let's take a look at what
achiral means, or not chiral.

00:05:47.109 --> 00:05:48.079
Achiral molecules.

00:05:48.269 --> 00:05:52.710
What this means is not chiral,
meaning that we're going to ignore

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:54.989
certain groups around a carbon.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:56.670
Now what are we ignoring?

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:00.590
Well, we want to ignore things
with three or less unique groups.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.650
So we need to have four things.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:05.959
So it has to be tetrahedral
and all four have to be unique.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:09.010
So we want to ignore
things like CH3, right?

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.509
Because there are three hydrogens.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:12.180
CH2, that's two hydrogens.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:16.690
Double bonds, double methyls,
symmetry, things like that.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.130
Now why is that?

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:22.710
Well, both of these you can see I have
two teal sections of this molecule.

00:06:23.159 --> 00:06:24.829
Meaning, that we have three unique groups.

00:06:25.219 --> 00:06:27.389
Well, can't you say, well they
look completely different?

00:06:27.469 --> 00:06:28.299
They should be chiral.

00:06:28.739 --> 00:06:33.260
However, one thing that you can do
is, if I turn this Notice how they

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:38.159
are completely the same, and if I try
to overlap them, well, their white

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.590
and yellow also overlaps as well.

00:06:41.039 --> 00:06:46.210
So we have not only achiral molecules,
but they're the same exact thing.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.950
You're able to overlap achiral as well.

00:06:49.189 --> 00:06:51.820
It doesn't have that optical property.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:54.530
Let's take a look at some
different examples and see how

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:56.460
we can apply this to molecules.

00:06:56.974 --> 00:07:01.484
Looking at molecules is a lot easier
when you are using models, and that's

00:07:01.484 --> 00:07:06.044
something that's a very harsh reality for
any student that is beginning at organic

00:07:06.065 --> 00:07:08.255
chemistry, or in chemistry in general.

00:07:08.844 --> 00:07:11.815
One of the things that we try to teach
our students is that you need to be

00:07:11.815 --> 00:07:15.844
able to visualize these molecules and
try to see what they really look like.

00:07:16.224 --> 00:07:19.534
We might write them on a sheet
